    _MISSING = object()  # unique sentinel: cannot be produced by any source

    def __init__(self, source):
        self._it = iter(source)
        self._cached = self._MISSING  # no prefetch in __init__
        self._exhausted = False

    def __iter__(self):
        return self

    def __next__(self):
        if self._cached is not self._MISSING:
            item = self._cached
            self._cached = self._MISSING
            return item
        if self._exhausted:
            raise StopIteration
        try:
            item = next(self._it)
        except StopIteration:
            self._exhausted = True
            raise
        return item

    def peek(self):
        if self._cached is not self._MISSING:
            return self._cached
        if self._exhausted:
            raise StopIteration
        try:
            item = next(self._it)
        except StopIteration:
            self._exhausted = True
            raise
        self._cached = item
        return item", "explanation": "Peekable caches one item lazily. __init__ does not prefetch (source_calls starts at 0). peek() pulls from the source only when no item is cached; the pulled item is stored via the unique sentinel _MISSING (a fresh object() no source can yield), so None is a valid cached item. Exhaustion (StopIteration from source) sets _exhausted=True, so later peek/next raise StopIteration without calling the source again. Other exceptions (e.g. ValueError) propagate and nothing is cached, so the next peek calls the source again. Trace for the given source: peek->None (call 1), peek->None (cached), next->None (consumes cache), peek->ValueError (call 2, propagates, not cached), peek->7 (call 3, cached), next->7, next->StopIteration (call 4, marks exhausted), peek->StopIteration (no source call). Total source_calls = 4. Limitation: assumes source's iterator protocol; only single-item lookahead."}

